\image(figures/dirichlet_regression.svg)
// result_end

The sediments call for both a curved mean and a depth-dependent precision. Adding \(\text{depth}^2\) to the mean model improves the likelihood by \(\chi^2_2=27.1\). Letting the precision depend on depth adds a further \(\chi^2_1=33.1\), and AIC falls by 31. The mean coefficients describe log-odds relative to clay. Sand falls steeply against clay with depth, and the positive quadratic term flattens the decline below about 60 m. Silt changes less, so the expected composition moves from sand-dominated near the shore (60% sand at 10 m) to an even silt–clay mixture in deep water. The precision rises from about 6 at 10 m to 245 at 100 m. Shallow samples vary widely, with a standard deviation of 0.11 in the clay share, while deep samples are almost uniform (0.03). The figure shows this narrowing scatter.

The logistic-normal regression, least squares on ilr coordinates, estimates something different. Its fitted values are compositional centers, the closed geometric means. Small shares pull a geometric mean toward zero. At 10 m the center puts clay at 3.1%, while the Dirichlet expected share is 8.4%. The simulation shows that this is not sampling noise: with the Dirichlet model as the truth, the Dirichlet fit recovers the expected clay share at 10 m (0.084), but the logistic-normal center stays near 0.05 whether n = 39 or 150. Neither estimate is wrong, but only the Dirichlet mean answers "what fraction is expected". Use the center when the question concerns typical ratios.

When the model matches the data, the observed-information Wald intervals cover 93–94% of the time, even with 39 observations. Ignoring the precision model is costly. A constant-precision fit treats the noisy shallow samples and the precise deep ones as equally informative, so its standard errors for the mean coefficients are wrong. Their coverage falls to 80% at n = 39, and to 57% with 150 observations, because more data concentrate the intervals around a misweighted estimate. The likelihood-ratio test detects the precision effect in every replicate, so modelling the precision costs little.

>> Practical guidance

- Model the precision explicitly and test covariates in it by likelihood ratio. Variable dispersion is common in compositional data, and ignoring it invalidates the inference for the mean model even when the mean itself is well estimated.
- Interpret \(\beta_j\) as log-odds of part \(j\) against the base part, and report fitted compositions at representative covariate values. Changing the base reparametrizes the model without changing the fit.
- Choose between the Dirichlet mean and the logistic-normal center according to the question: expected shares or typical ratios. The Dirichlet model also has a single precision governing all parts, so a covariance structure among log-ratios calls for logistic-normal models.
- Zeros make Dirichlet log-likelihoods infinite. Boundary compression is a pragmatic fix for a few zeros, but frequent zeros call for a zero-inflated or multinomial-count model.

>> References

- M. J. Maier (2014). DirichletReg: Dirichlet regression for compositional data in R. Research Report Series 125, Vienna University of Economics and Business.
- R. H. Hijazi and R. W. Jernigan (2009). Modelling compositional data using Dirichlet regression models. *Journal of Applied Probability and Statistics* 4(1), 77-91.
- J. P. Coakley and B. R. Rust (1968). Sedimentation in an Arctic lake. *Journal of Sedimentary Petrology* 38(4), 1290-1300. doi:10.1306/74D71B5B-2B21-11D7-8648000102C1865D
- J. Aitchison (1986). *The Statistical Analysis of Compositional Data*. Chapman and Hall. doi:10.1007/978-94-009-4109-0
- S. Ferrari and F. Cribari-Neto (2004). Beta regression for modelling rates and proportions. *Journal of Applied Statistics* 31(7), 799-815. doi:10.1080/0266476042000214501
- M. Smithson and J. Verkuilen (2006). A better lemon squeezer? Maximum-likelihood regression with beta-distributed dependent variables. *Psychological Methods* 11(1), 54-71. doi:10.1037/1082-989X.11.1.54
